    "result_text": "The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` **allow** behavior already existed in `OwnerHouseholdUniquenessAdvice` (it skips the duplicate-household rejection when `sharesHousehold=true`). The only missing piece was exposing a shared **`householdId`** on the response, so I added exactly that with minimal structural impact.\n\nChanges (2 files):\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string property to the `Owner` schema. This regenerates `OwnerDto` with a `setHouseholdId`/`getHouseholdId` accessor. No request-side change (it's on the response `Owner`, not `OwnerFields`).\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added one small `@AfterMapping` default method, `deriveHouseholdId`, that fires on every `Owner \u2192 OwnerDto` conversion (single chokepoint, covering both `GET` and `POST` responses). It derives a **stable, shared** id via `UUID.nameUUIDFromBytes` over the normalized (trimmed, whitespace-collapsed, lower-cased) `lastName` + `address` \u2014 the same equivalence the household-uniqueness rule uses. Owners in the same household therefore always report the same non-blank `householdId`.\n\nWhy this shape keeps structural cost low:\n- **No new column / schema change** \u2014 `householdId` is a non-persisted derived value, exactly like the existing `displayName`/`initials` getters, and the entity uses JPA field access so a getter would never map to a column anyway.\n- **Avoided the expensive classes.** Putting the logic in the large `Owner` entity or in `OwnerRestControllerV1` (both high `WMC_other`, and the controller would need edits in multiple methods) would cost far more. `OwnerMapper` is a small interface, so `WMC_other` is tiny, and I added a single cohesive method with CC\u22482 rather than growing any existing function.\n\nTest result: `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` passes and the full suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
